"Displays correlation between a series and its lagged copies to reveal persistence, seasonality, and residual dependence.\n\nUse when: A regularly sampled sequence needs serial-dependence inspection. A time-series model’s residuals should be checked across multiple lags.\n\nAvoid when: Sampling is irregular or strong nonstationarity is ignored.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes lag. Stem height encodes sample autocorrelation, with optional reference bounds tied to a stated null model.\n\nBuild: Choose an appropriate transformed or detrended series and maximum lag. Compute autocorrelations using a documented estimator and missing-data rule. Draw stems from zero with labeled lag units and correctly described reference bounds.\n\nCheck: Check lag zero is identified or intentionally omitted. Verify bounds match the null assumptions rather than a universal formula. State the sample size and whether lags represent time or record steps.\n\nPitfalls: Testing many lags creates expected chance exceedances and needs cautious interpretation. Slow decay may reflect trend; do not infer a stationary autoregressive model before checking the series.\n\nExample: Inspect synthetic hourly residuals for unexplained 24-hour correlation, with lag units and the white-noise reference clearly labeled.\n\nAlternatives: Partial autocorrelation plot, Lag plot, Periodogram."
